● Good question — this is exactly the distinction the report needs to make in the identification section. The short answer: the lasso
  selects variables that predict well, not variables that are causally admissible. Those are different properties, and the lasso cannot
  tell them apart.

  Take the two cases separately, because they're omitted for completely different reasons:

  1. industry_public and fleet_petrol_share — pure mechanics, not causal. The four industry shares sum to exactly 1 for every city, so
  any one of them is a perfect linear combination of the other three — it carries literally zero additional information. Same for the
  three fleet shares. This is the same reason model.matrix drops one category of every factor (the "dummy variable trap"). It's not that
  these variables are dangerous; you drop one arbitrary reference category and nothing is lost. The choice of which one to drop doesn't
  matter at all.

  2. pm25, the fleet shares, total_co2 — the causal problem, and here your intuition ("lasso will zero them out") fails in the worst
  possible way. The lasso keeps whatever predicts strongly. Now ask: does fleet_diesel_share predict emissions? Extremely well — it's
  the main channel through which a LEZ works: LEZ → fewer diesel cars → lower emissions. So the lasso would not zero it out; it would
  love it. And then the partialling-out step removes all variation in emissions explained by fleet composition — which includes most of
  the LEZ effect itself. Your θ_lez would answer "what does a LEZ do holding the vehicle fleet fixed?", which is close to zero by
  construction, because changing the fleet is what the LEZ does. Same logic for pm25 (caused by traffic, so it's downstream of your
  outcome) and total_co2 (arithmetically contains your outcome).
  
    So the division of labor is:

  - You (economics, before any estimation) decide the admissible set: exclude anything caused by the treatment (mediators: fleet
  shares), caused by the outcome (pm25), containing the outcome (total_co2), or derived from the treatment (the timing variables). This
  requires knowing the causal story; no algorithm can do it, because these variables look great statistically.
  - The lasso (within the admissible set) decides which of the many harmless candidates actually matter. Flagpoles and benches are safe
  to throw in precisely because they're not caused by the policies or the emissions — worst case they're irrelevant, and irrelevance is
  the one problem lasso genuinely solves.

  The practical decision rule per variable, in order:
  1. Is it Y, or does it contain Y? → out.
  2. Is it the treatment or a function of it (timing vars)? → out (or it is D).
  3. Could the policy plausibly change it? → mediator, out of the baseline.
  4. Otherwise → into W, let the lasso decide
  
  
  
  
❯ the timing variables for announcement as well as implemented year seem to be important but are not uses because of this problem right

● Almost — right instinct, but the problem is even more direct than the mediator issue, and they are used, just not as controls.

  cp_impl_year isn't a mediator (nothing the policy causes) — it is the treatment, in a different encoding. Together with year,
  cp_impl_year determines cp_active exactly: cp_active = 1(year ≥ cp_impl_year > 0), and build_W already puts year dummies in W. So if
  you added the implementation years to W, the first-stage lasso could predict your treatment from the controls almost perfectly.
  Remember what DML does with that: it regresses residualized Y on residualized D. If W explains D nearly completely, the D residuals
  are ~zero — there's no variation left to estimate θ from, and the estimate blows up or becomes meaningless. You'd be "controlling for"
  the very thing whose effect you want.

  The announcement years are the softer version of the same issue — they're an early symptom of the city's decision to adopt, so
  partialling them out also eats treatment variation (and any anticipation effect with it).

  But your feeling that they're important is correct, and the code does exploit them — in the two places where they belong:

  1. Placebo test (04, spec5): the announcement years define "announced but not yet implemented" city-years (anticip_cp, anticip_lez,
  built in 01_data_preparation.R:30-35). These enter as extra treatment columns, not controls. If their coefficients are ~0, that says
  announcement alone doesn't move emissions — evidence against anticipation behavior and against selection-on-trends around adoption.
  2. Event study (04, spec6): the implementation years define event time (rel_cp, rel_lez = years since implementation), which gives
  the leads-and-lags plot. Flat pre-implementation coefficients are your best available evidence that treated and untreated cities
  weren't already diverging before the policy — the closest thing to testing the identification assumption.

  So the clean way to say it in the report: the timing variables are informative about when treatment happens, and information about
  treatment assignment belongs on the D side of the model (as placebo treatments or event-time dummies), never on the W side — controls
  must be things that could confound the policy, not things that encode it.
  
  
  